<BaseLayout title="Quality is an architectural property · Santiago Madriz">
  <header class="page-intro" data-reveal><div class="eyebrow">Engineering note · 5 min read</div><h1>Quality is an architectural property.</h1><p>A test suite can report on a system. It cannot compensate for a system that was never designed to be understood.</p></header>
  <article class="prose" data-reveal>
    <p>Teams often discuss quality at the end of the delivery sequence: implementation is complete, so now somebody should test it. That framing makes quality a gate. In practice, the most reliable systems make quality decisions much earlier.</p>
    <h2>Testable boundaries are design boundaries</h2><p>If a pricing rule can only be verified through a full browser checkout, the problem is not missing test automation. The rule is trapped inside an expensive boundary. Moving it into an explicit, deterministic unit improves design and makes fast feedback possible.</p>
    <h2>Failure behavior is product behavior</h2><p>Timeouts, invalid input, missing content, and partial dependencies are not unusual exceptions. They are states the product will eventually enter. Defining those states makes the experience more predictable and the implementation easier to test.</p>
    <h2>Observability closes the loop</h2><p>Pre-release confidence has a limit. Health signals, useful logs, request context, and real performance measurements tell us how the deployed system behaves. Without that feedback, a green pipeline is only evidence about a controlled environment.</p>
    <blockquote>Quality engineering is the work of making confidence intentional, from design through production.</blockquote>
    <h2>A practical standard</h2><p>For every meaningful change, I want to know which boundary owns it, how invalid states are rejected, what automated evidence protects it, and how a production failure would become visible. Those questions lead to simpler systems—and better tests.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
